From b22982e508b173c5da500b911249215a6fd7500b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Cipoletti <adam@amcreativecoach.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 11:05:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 57/58] Hold the visible content rectangle for the length of a
 layout or a paint

A fresh profile of TodoMVC-JavaScript-ES5 put 1.5% of the main thread in
objc_msgSend_stret and its dyld stub, under -[NSScrollView
documentVisibleRect] and -[NSClipView documentVisibleRect], reached through
ScrollView::platformVisibleContentRect. Every repaint rectangle computed
during layout asks where the view is visible, and each answer is an
Objective-C call returning a structure, guarded against Objective-C
exceptions, plus a floorf and a ceilf in enclosingIntRect.

Neither layout nor painting can move the view while it runs, so FrameView
freezes the answer for the length of each pass and the platform code caches
it - but only while frozen, so a stale rectangle cannot outlive the pass that
asked for it. Outside those passes nothing is cached and every caller still
asks AppKit.

TodoMVC-JavaScript-ES5 goes from about 5332 to a mean of 5126 ms over four
runs (5057 to 5223), which is 3.9%, against a run-to-run spread of 1.6%.

While measuring this the assumption behind the next piece of work also fell
over: HTML parsing is 2.4% of that suite here, not the 20% Apple reported for
their own baseline, so porting the fast-path parser would buy almost nothing.
The profile's real costs are cross-cutting: GCC's out-of-line register
restore helper at 2.9%, pthread_getspecific under bmalloc at 2.2%, floorf and
round through dyld stubs at 2%.
